Routing [Track]
To facilitate a branching Track, can use any of these controls to move a Transporter on to the succeeding
object.
Remarks
Plant Simulation uses:
• The Exit Control of the Track.
• Automatic Routing of the Transporter.
• The Driving Control of the Transporter.
• The built-in properties of the Track described below.
The Exit Control of the Track has the highest priority. If you did not define an Exit Control and if you
typed in a destination list for the Transporter, Automatic Routing moves the Transporter on to the proper
successor, using the destination lists of the successors.
You can also assign a Destination to the Transporter. The Track has a Forward Destination List and a
Backward Destination List, into which you type all destinations that the Transporter can reach when it
drives on the Track. If you assigned a destination list to the Transporter that is ready to be moved on,
the Track searches the Forward Destination List and the Backward Destination List of all successors for
the Destination that you typed in, and, as soon as it finds it, moves the Transporter on to that successor.
If the Track does not find the destination, it moves the Transporter to the next successor in turn. Plant
Simulation terminates the search after it finds the first instance of the Destination. Automatic routing is
inactive if you did not type in a Destination for the Transporter or if you typed in an Exit Control for the
Track.
If you neither typed in an Exit Control nor any Destination Lists for the successors, the Track uses the
Driving Control of the Transporter to move it. If you do not type in any controls at all, the built-in
properties of Track move the Transporters to each of the successors, to which it is connected, in turn.
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
